    "question": {
      ":question/id": "7d0decaf-7fa1-56ca-be0f-b7a7799976a8",
      ":question/problem": "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=- \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} &=5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The third equation states that ${x}_{3}=5.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-(5)=- \\frac{3}{2}\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=- \\frac{3}{2}-(-5)=\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{2}=\\frac{7}{2}$ and ${x}_{3}=5$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-2(\\frac{7}{2})-(5)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}-(-7)-(-5)=\\frac{25}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{25}{2}$, ${x}_{2}=\\frac{7}{2}$, and ${x}_{3}=5.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{25}{2} \\\\ \\frac{7}{2} \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
